@meta
id: 21-01
title: Record once, play it back
chapter: 21
type: lesson
skills: macro-record, macro-play
par: 45
minutes: 6
vscode: eight cursors with Ctrl+D, End, type, and hope the paste lands
open: order-status.ts

@brief
`OrderStatus` is a numeric enum and the API has started leaking the
numbers. Every member needs an explicit string value equal to its own
name:

    PENDING = 'PENDING',

Eight members. On one line the edit is a yank of the name, an append, a
paste and a bit of punctuation. `.` cannot hold that: it is two inserts
and a yank, not one change.

You know from chapter 20 that a register is a box of keystrokes. This
chapter is about filling one by *performing* the edit, then running the
box on every other line. Vim calls the box a macro. It is nothing more
than the register you already know, with a recorder pointed at it.

@keys
| `qa` | start recording into register `a` |
| `q` | stop recording |
| `@a` | play register `a` as keystrokes |
| `^` `$` | first non-blank, end of line: the macro moves to known columns |

@hints
Press `qa`, do the edit on `PENDING` exactly as you would by hand, move
down a line, press `q`. Then `@a` on each remaining line. `recording @a`
shows at the bottom while the recorder is on.
--
The line already ends in a comma, so drop it first with `$x`. Then `^`
and `yiw` to take the name, `A = '` and `Esc`, `p` puts the name back
after the quote, `a',` and `Esc`, then `j` so the next `@a` starts on
the next line. The `x` has to come before the yank: a delete lands in
the unnamed register too, and it would overwrite the name.
--
`j` then `qa$x^yiwA = '<Esc>pa',<Esc>jq`, then `@a` seven times.

@solution
    j  qa $x ^yiw A = ' <Esc> p a', <Esc> j q   @a @a @a @a @a @a @a   :wq<Enter>

Forty keystrokes, and the recording is most of them. Each replay
is two.

Three things to notice. First, the `$` at the front and the `^` after
it. When you press `qa` the cursor is wherever it is; when `@a` runs on
a later line it is wherever the previous `j` left it. A macro that
begins by moving to a known column works from anywhere. A macro that
assumes the column is the one you recorded from works once.

Second, the `j` at the end. It is not part of the edit. It is there so
that the macro leaves the cursor where the next macro needs to start.
The recording is a unit of *work plus setup for the next unit*, and
that is the design habit this chapter keeps coming back to.

Third, you did not have to plan. You did the edit once, slowly, looking
at the screen, and the recorder took notes. If you make a mistake while
recording, press `q`, `u` until the line is clean, and record again.
Nothing is lost but a few seconds.

`:reg a` shows what was captured. It reads exactly as you typed it,
with `^[` standing for `Esc`. You will use that view later in the
chapter to fix a macro instead of re-recording it.

@start order-status.ts
export enum OrderStatus {
  PENDING,
  CONFIRMED,
  PAID,
  PACKED,
  SHIPPED,
  DELIVERED,
  CANCELLED,
  REFUNDED,
}

@expected order-status.ts
export enum OrderStatus {
  PENDING = 'PENDING',
  CONFIRMED = 'CONFIRMED',
  PAID = 'PAID',
  PACKED = 'PACKED',
  SHIPPED = 'SHIPPED',
  DELIVERED = 'DELIVERED',
  CANCELLED = 'CANCELLED',
  REFUNDED = 'REFUNDED',
}
